# The font stylesheets are hashed into public/font/fonts-manifest.json, and
# that hash is the cache-buster for the stylesheet URLs. The hash has to be
# the same on every platform, so their line endings are pinned to LF: with
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout would otherwise rewrite them to
# CRLF and the manifest would no longer match the file it names.
#
# ⚠️ Every family has TWO stylesheets since the ext tier was split out of
# the render-blocking one (2026-11-18, commit 0c08b0587): the hot tier and
# the ext tier. Both go into the hash, so BOTH need the pin.
#
# ⚠️ A pattern that matches no file protects NOTHING — and the rewrite it
# was written to prevent comes back silently, because `git status` reports
# the file as clean (autocrlf normalizes it back to LF on the way in). The
# test `tests/unit/deploy/font-stylesheet-eol.test.ts` asserts that every
# stylesheet the manifest names is pinned, and that no pin names a file
# that does not exist.
public/font/fonts-sans-hot.css text eol=lf
public/font/fonts-sans-ext.css text eol=lf
public/font/fonts-serif-hot.css text eol=lf
public/font/fonts-serif-ext.css text eol=lf
public/font/fonts-wenkai-hot.css text eol=lf
public/font/fonts-wenkai-ext.css text eol=lf

# The subsets are binary. Git already detects them as binary (the WOFF2
# signature has a NUL byte at offset 4), but say so rather than rely on it.
public/font/web/** binary
